#v(0.5cm)

You may feel a tightening in your chest as you hold this journal. Perhaps a quiet voice whispers, _"Not now. Not yet. I'm not ready."_ Let me tell you something that took me years to understand: that voice is not your enemy. It is your psyche's ancient gatekeeper, and it has been protecting you your entire life.

What you hold in your hands is not a self-help book. It is an invitation to descend—gently, at your own pace—into the garden of your own soul.

#v(0.3cm)

#align(center)[#text(size: 12pt)[◈ · ✦ · ◈]]

#v(0.3cm)

Carl Jung taught us that the psyche is not a machine to be fixed, but a garden to be tended. Within it grows both nightshade and rose, poison and medicine, shadow and light. We have spent our lives learning which plants are acceptable—which emotions, desires, and parts of ourselves we may show to the world. The rest, we buried. We exiled them to the darkest corners of the garden, hoping they would wither and disappear.

They did not disappear. They grew roots.

The shadow is not your darkness. It is your _buried_ wholeness—the parts of you that were too bright, too wild, too tender, too true. They were not wrong. They were simply unsafe to express. And so you fragmented yourself to survive.

This journal will guide you to those forgotten corners. You will learn to name what lives there. You will feel where these exiled parts have taken residence in your body—in the clenched jaw, the tight throat, the heavy heart. And slowly, with great compassion, you will bring them home.

#v(0.3cm)

